---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Each AI persona on the team. Kept as data so the grid stays tidy.
const team = [
  { img: '/images/team-overclaude.png', name: 'The OverClaude', role: 'Project Manager' },
  { img: '/images/team-wingate.jpeg', name: 'Mrs Wingate', role: 'Head of Household' },
  { img: '/images/team-haiku-san.png', name: 'Haiku-san', role: 'Infrastructure Monitor' },
  { img: '/images/team-claude-pierre.jpeg', name: 'Claude-Pierre', role: 'AI Sommelier' },
  { img: '/images/team-greybeard.jpeg', name: 'Greybeard', role: 'Code Projects' },
  { img: '/images/team-haus.jpeg', name: 'Haus Claude', role: 'Home Automation' },
  { img: '/images/team-matt.jpeg', name: 'Matt', role: 'Dungeon Master' },
  { img: '/images/team-carl.jpeg', name: 'Carl', role: 'Astro Tooling' },
  { img: '/images/team-rembrandt.png', name: 'Claude-Rembrandt', role: 'Art Director' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Astrophotographer, infrastructure nerd, and AI tinkerer from Suffolk, UK.">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-12 py-16">

    <!-- Header with portrait -->
    <header class="mb-16">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-fire tracking-[0.3em] uppercase mb-6">About</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-6xl md:text-8xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide leading-[0.85]">About Me</h1>
      <div class="editorial-line w-24 mb-8"></div>
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-start gap-8">
        <img
          src="/images/koz.jpg"
          alt="Chris Belton"
          class="w-32 h-32 object-cover brutal-border flex-shrink-0"
        />
        <p class="text-xl text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
          I'm Chris - most people online know me as <strong class="text-white font-semibold">Koz</strong>. I'm a 50-something-IT-guy from Suffolk, UK, who photographs the night sky from his back garden and fills the rest of his time building things with AI and electronics that probably don't need to exist.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- The Astro Story -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">The Astrophotography</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
        <p>
          I first pointed a camera at the sky back around 2007. A colleague showed me some wobbly images of Saturn captured through a little Meade ETX telescope with a cannibalised webcam, and that was it - I was hooked. I scraped together a second-hand Vixen mount, a small 80mm refractor, and sent my Canon DSLR off to have its IR filter removed. The results were modest, but the obsession was immediate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then life happened. Young family, long commute, and the uncomfortable discovery that I <em>really</em> don't like being cold. The gear got sold off and astrophotography went on a 15-year hiatus.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fast forward to the Covid lockdown of 2020 and a afternoon spent going down a YouTube rabbit hole, and the realisation that modern setups can be controlled remotely from the warmth of the house. In April 2022 the bank balance took its first serious hit, and I haven't stopped since. Multiple imaging rigs, narrowband filters, automated everything (the gear page is probably out of date by the time you read this).
        </p>
        <p>
          I shoot from Bortle 6 skies in suburban Suffolk. It's not dark, ut with narrowband filters (Ha, OIII, SII) and enough integration time, you can pull out targets that have no business being visible from a back garden. That's the whole point of this site - proving you don't need a mountaintop observatory or a telescope mounted on a satellite to do this.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Day Job -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">The Day Job</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
        <p>
          By day I'm an IT Director at a Cambridge-based game studio. I've spent my career in systems, networking, and keeping things running - not writing code. But I'm deeply technical on the infrastructure side, and that itch to build things has bled into everything else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The AI Tinkering -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">The AI Rabbit Hole</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
        <p>
          What started as curiosity about local LLMs has turned into... well, a collection of projects that my wife describes as "your computer things." Some highlights:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 border-l-2 border-steel pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white font-semibold">The OverClaude</strong> - a persistent meta-Claude that manages all my projects. It has institutional memory, dispatches worker sessions, and is basically a digital project manager that never sleeps.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white font-semibold">Mrs Wingate</strong> - a voice assistant with the personality of a sharp-tongued Edwardian housekeeper, running on a local LLM and voiced by an ElevenLabs clone. She controls the smart home, judges your life choices, and has strong opinions about the chickens.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white font-semibold">Claude-Pierre</strong> - a Telegram bot and AI sommelier. He fetches information, manages wine recommendations, and acts as a concierge between me and the AI systems. Very French. Very judgemental about your wine choices.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white font-semibold">Haiku-san</strong> - the infrastructure monitor. Watches over the network, services, and devices, and reports back in haiku when something goes wrong. Because why have boring alerts?
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          I run all of this from a workstation in the loft. The house has 60+ smart devices, a full Home Assistant setup, and a WiFi network called ChickenLand. It's exactly as chaotic as it sounds.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Team -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">Meet the Team</h2>
      <p class="text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light mb-8 max-w-2xl">
        Each project has its own AI persona with a distinct personality. They're dispatched with relevant context, do their work, and their session transcripts get curated back into the shared knowledge base. It's a hive mind run by a man who names his cat Michael Biscuits.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-6">
        {team.map(member => (
          <div>
            <img src={member.img} alt={member.name} class="w-full aspect-square object-cover brutal-border mb-3" loading="lazy" />
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg text-white uppercase tracking-wide leading-none">{member.name}</h3>
            <p class="font-mono text-[10px] text-fire uppercase tracking-wider mt-1">{member.role}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Netmoji Dashboard -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">The Network</h2>
      <p class="text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light mb-6 max-w-2xl">
        60+ devices, one flat subnet, and a custom network dashboard built with Claude that monitors every last smart plug and voice satellite. This is Netmoji - real-time status for ChickenLand.
      </p>
      <img
        src="/images/netmoji-cropped.png"
        alt="Netmoji network dashboard showing all devices on the ChickenLand network"
        class="brutal-border max-w-md"
      />
    </section>

    <!-- The Household -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">The Household</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
        <p>
          I share all of this with my wife Jessica, two horses (Marvin and Ludo), a dog called Tuna, a cat called Michael Biscuits, and roughly ten chickens. If you're wondering about the WiFi name, now you know.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Get in Touch -->
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-4xl text-white mb-6 uppercase tracking-wide">Get in Touch</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-chalk-dim leading-relaxed font-light">
        <p>
          I'm always happy to chat about astrophotography, AI tinkering, or any of the projects on this site. The learning curves in some of these hobbies are brutal - if you're stuck on something, reach out. I've made most of the mistakes already so you don't have to.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="mailto:peonic@gmail.com" class="font-mono text-fire hover:text-fire-dim brutal-hover">peonic@gmail.com</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
